import std.dl

# Parameters may appear inside choice alternatives. When the call matches the
# alternative containing the parameter, it binds like any argument. When the
# call matches another alternative, the parameter behaves like a fresh local
# of that instantiation.

function the combination of alpha [with beta|]:
	execute:
		return alpha + beta

print (the combination of 1 with 2) as a line

function run count iterations [starting at frame|from the beginning]:
	execute:
		set frame to 0
		loop while frame < count:
			increment frame
		print frame as a line

run 3 iterations from the beginning
run 5 iterations from the beginning

# The bound instantiation of the same definition still works alongside the
# unbound one: here the second argument binds frame through the alternative.
run 4 iterations starting at 9

# A choice parameter in the middle of the pattern, unbound through the word
# alternative and bound through the parameter alternative.
function the middle result [from gap|by default] with tail:
	execute:
		set gap to tail * 2
		return gap

print (the middle result by default with 6) as a line
print (the middle result from 5 with 7) as a line
